<https://w3id.org/gelmat/Aerogel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An aerogel is a porous solid material obtained by replacing the liquid phase of a gel with a gas, typically through supercritical drying, while largely retaining the gel’s original network structure."@en .
<https://w3id.org/gelmat/Aerogel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Aerogel"@en .
<https://w3id.org/gelmat/Aerogel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#closeMatch> <http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_4207e895> .
<https://w3id.org/gelmat/Aerogel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#closeMatch> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/Material> .
<https://w3id.org/gelmat/Aerogel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A porous solid material derived from a gel in which the liquid component has been replaced by a gas without significant collapse of the solid network structure, yielding extremely low density, high porosity, and high specific surface area"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/gelmat/AerogelDefect
<https://w3id.org/gelmat/AerogelDefect> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/gelmat/AerogelDefect> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/polymat/Feature> .
<https://w3id.org/gelmat/AerogelDefect> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A structural or quality defect borne by an aerogel — a persisting deviation from the intended structure or quality that arises during synthesis or processing. Subclasses denote the defect state (a specifically dependent continuant inhering in the material), not the formation event; the event that produces a defect should be modelled as a process if needed."@en .
<https://w3id.org/gelmat/AerogelDefect> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Aerogel defect"@en .
<https://w3id.org/gelmat/AerogelDefect> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A specifically dependent continuant feature of an aerogel material that constitutes a deviation from the intended structure, composition, or quality of the material"@en .
